<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berghof</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hitler's Berghof</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Berghof was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>'s mountain residence on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obersalzberg">Obersalzberg</a> above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berchtesgaden">Berchtesgaden</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Rebuilt from a holiday house in 1935 and 1936, it was where he spent much of his time and received foreign visitors, including the Austrian chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-schuschnigg">Kurt Schuschnigg</a> and the British prime minister Neville Chamberlain in 1938. Damaged by bombing in April 1945, its ruins were demolished in 1952.</p></div>
